+#!/usr/bin/env python3
+"""Does the PUBLISHED explorer stop on the right quantity, and does the proposed
+replacement terminate?
+
+Target: `explorer/index.html`, served at
+jebus197.github.io/Constraint_Engineering/explorer/. Line 83 labels its slider
+`theta  stopping threshold on dR`; line 294 colours a pass green on
+`s.dR >= o.theta` alone and line 304 prints `stop ~= pass N`. That makes dR a
+DECISION RULE, not merely a trajectory display.
+
+THREE RESULTS, ALL DERIVED HERE RATHER THAN ASSERTED.
+
+1. THE RECURSION IS CORRECT AND MUST NOT BE TOUCHED. `simulate()` at
+   index.html:164-180 is line-for-line the appendix's canonical Stage 5 three
+   phases (docs/MATHEMATICAL_APPENDIX.md:248, :250, :254, :274), and :264-268
+   DEFENDS the conditional Phase-2 blend as deliberately conservative with z3
+   unsat. A fix that rewrote the trajectory would contradict the appendix.
+   What is wrong is the quantity the STOP is taken on, nothing else.
+
+2. THE STOP QUANTITY IS THE RETIRED ONE, AND THE ERROR IS STRICTLY
+   ONE-DIRECTIONAL -- with a closed form, not a grid count. At sigma=1, nu=0 the
+   explorer's `dR` reduces EXACTLY to the retired `q*R*(1-R)/(1-q*R)`. Against
+   :225's expected improvement the gap is
+
+       E_general - dR_explorer = R^2 * q * sigma * (1-nu) * (1-q) / (1 - R*q)
+
+   every factor of which is positive on the open box, so the explorer can only
+   stop EARLY, never late. That expression is the appendix's own already-verified
+   Phase-2 conservatism gap at :266 multiplied by (1-nu) -- the same identity,
+   extended through Phase 3. Astra's proposed `R*q` is also wrong, and :223 says
+   why in terms: it is the sigma=1, nu=0 corner, and the explorer's own sliders
+   default to sigma=0.9, nu=0.05.
+
+3. THE OBVIOUS FIX DOES NOT FIX -- IT REMOVES TERMINATION. Substituting :225's
+   E_general for dR on the CONDITIONAL trajectory never stops, because at that
+   trajectory's fixed point R* = nu/(q*(sigma+nu*(1-sigma))) -- the value the
+   explorer already draws at index.html:258 and labels "floor actually reached"
+   -- dR is 0 by construction while
+
+       E_general(R*) = nu^2 * (1-q) / (q * (sigma + nu*(1-sigma)))  >  0.
+
+   So the revised rule reports gain available at the exact state where the
+   displayed trajectory has provably stopped moving. Measured below over the
+   explorer's own slider ranges.
+
+   THE COHERENT FIX IS THREE CLAUSES, EVERY ONE ALREADY IN THE APPENDIX AND
+   ALREADY COMPUTED BY THE EXPLORER. Continue only while ALL THREE hold:
+
+       (1)  E_general  >=  theta          appendix :225 -- the right quantity
+       (2)  R - R_floor >= theta          index.html:258 -- gain that is ATTAINABLE
+       (3)  nu < nu*(R)                   appendix :280/:284 -- hard divergence exit
+
+   Clause 1 alone was my first attempt and IT FAILED ITS OWN SWEEP, 1450 of
+   27000. Clauses 2 and 3 are complementary and both are needed:
+     - In the CONVERGENT regime the trajectory approaches R_floor asymptotically
+       and never attains it, so nu* stays above nu by ~1e-10 forever and clause 3
+       never fires; clause 2 fires because no pass can take R below the floor.
+     - In the DIVERGENT regime (nu > nu*, the loop pinned at R = 1) R_floor is
+       outside [0,1] so the explorer does not draw it and clause 2 has nothing to
+       cap against; clause 3 fires, which is exactly the appendix's own
+       "stop fixing and report the finding for human review".
+   Note rStar > 1  <=>  nu > nu*(R=1), so the two regimes partition the box and
+   the pair is exhaustive by construction, not by luck.
+
+Open-source tools only, by the stored-falsifier rule: no Wolfram call here, so
+anyone reproducing this project does not need it installed.
+
+Run:  python3 scripts/explorer_stopping_rule_2026-09-28.py
+Exit: 0 if every derivation and every sweep holds, 1 on the first that does not.
+"""
